# vtium's configuration, installed as /etc/vtium/vtium.conf.example.
# Generated from crates/vtium/src/config/keys.rs; docs/config.md describes each key.
#
# One key a line: key=value, or a switch's key to turn it on and no-key to
# turn it off. Each key is shown at its default, commented out. The command
# line wins over this file.

## Log debug messages as well, to find what went wrong. Off, vtium logs its information messages, warnings and errors.
#no-debug

## The VT to run on: a number N for `/dev/ttyN`, a name under `/dev`, or a path. Unset, the VT vtium was started on, or the next free one.
#vt=

## Switch to vtium's VT at start; off, wait until something else switches to it.
#switchvt

## The session's command. Unset, `/bin/login -p` with `reset-env` on and `/bin/login` with it off. A word that is `$NAME` becomes that variable of the session's environment, and `$$` starts a word with `$`.
#login=

## Exit when the session's command exits, in place of starting it again.
#no-oneshot

## The most consoles one head holds: the head file's `consoles` opens no more.
#head-consoles-max=4

## The most consoles every head holds together. Each head keeps its first console; the others open, in the head file's order, while the total is below this.
#consoles-max=8

## What vtium's on-screen display draws at the top of each head after a change. With `minimal`, it draws a dot in the top-right cell, filled on the active head and hollow on the others. With `full`, it draws a line across the top, such as `● desk · console 2 of 3`. With `off`, it draws nothing. A notice of a problem shows whatever this key says.
#osd=minimal

## How long the on-screen display stays after a change, counted from the first frame that draws it. `never` keeps it up from the start.
#osd-timeout=3

## The older name of `osd` and `osd-timeout`. vtium reads it only where `osd` or `osd-timeout` is not set: `auto` reads as `osd = minimal`, `always` as `osd = minimal` with `osd-timeout = never`, and `off` as `osd = off`. vtium logs a line naming the new keys when a file or the command line sets it.
#focus-mark=

## The color of a notice's text. A notice tells of a problem, such as an invalid head file, and shows whatever `osd` says.
#notice-foreground=31,41,51

## The color of a notice's box. While a notice shows, the on-screen display's full line shares its box and takes the notice's colors.
#notice-background=254,226,226

## The color of the edge around a notice's box.
#notice-border=185,28,28

## The color of the on-screen display's text: the words of `osd = full`'s line, and the dot of `osd = minimal`.
#osd-foreground=229,231,235

## The color of the on-screen display's box behind `osd = full`'s line, and of the cell behind `osd = minimal`'s dot.
#osd-background=31,41,51

## The color of the edge around `osd = full`'s box. The dot of `osd = minimal` has no edge.
#osd-border=97,110,124

## Other schemes for the six colors above. To use one, remove the # at the start of its six lines. vtium draws whatever colors are set and checks no contrast between a text and its box.

## light: For a light terminal background: the on-screen display is a pale grey box with dark text and a grey edge, and a notice a dark red box with white text and a darker edge, so a problem differs from a status in brightness as well as in hue.
#notice-foreground=255,255,255
#notice-background=153,27,27
#notice-border=69,10,10
#osd-foreground=31,41,51
#osd-background=243,244,246
#osd-border=156,163,175

## dark: Both boxes are dark, for a dark terminal background: a notice is deep red with pale text and a light red edge, and the on-screen display near black with light grey text and a grey edge.
#notice-foreground=254,226,226
#notice-background=185,28,28
#notice-border=248,113,113
#osd-foreground=209,213,219
#osd-background=17,24,39
#osd-border=75,85,99

## color-blind-safe: For color blindness, a problem differs from a status in brightness and in an orange and blue pair, never in red against green: a notice is a bright orange box with near-black text, and the on-screen display a dark blue box with near-white text.
#notice-foreground=28,25,23
#notice-background=255,176,0
#notice-border=254,97,0
#osd-foreground=238,242,255
#osd-background=11,42,91
#osd-border=100,143,255

## `TERM` in the session's environment. The default names vtium's own terminfo entry, and XTGETTCAP answers from that entry's capabilities; `kmscon` names the C reference's entry, and XTGETTCAP answers from the C reference's. Under any other name every name XTGETTCAP asks for is unknown.
#term=xterm-vtium

## Start the session with an empty environment and the variables vtium sets; off, with vtium's own environment as well.
#reset-env

## The pty's erase character is DEL (0x7f); off, BS (0x08).
#backspace-delete

## Lines of scrollback, at most `sb-size-cap`.
#sb-size=1000

## The most lines `sb-size` may ask for. Unset, it follows the machine's memory: 10,000 lines below 2 GiB, 30,000 from 2 GiB and 100,000 from 4 GiB.
#sb-size-cap=

## The most bytes the scrollback holds, or KiB, MiB or GiB with `K`, `M` or `G` (`6M`), at most a quarter of the machine's memory, or the default when `/proc/meminfo` cannot be read; its oldest lines go past it, as past `sb-size`. Left unset, the default follows each console's grid, worked out again when the grid changes: on a grid up to minspec's 170 by 48 it is what the 16 MiB minspec peak leaves after the console's two screens at their worst and the rest of vtium, so a smaller display keeps more, and a larger display keeps the 170 by 48 value shown here. The default does not grow with the display or the machine's memory; on a machine that can spare more, set `sb-memory` for a deeper scrollback, and it stays fixed.
#sb-memory=5242880

## Rings the VT's bell when a program sends BEL. A program stops its own BELs ringing by resetting mode 1042, which starts set. Off, no BEL rings.
#no-bell

## Blink text with SGR 5 and a blinking cursor, every 500 ms; off, neither blinks.
#blink

## How text with SGR 5 or 6 draws in the blink's off phase: `full` hides it, `partial` draws it halfway to its background as SGR 2 does, and `off` draws it steady. The cursor blinks as `blink` says either way.
#blink-text=full

## The underline and strike of blinking text blink with it: hidden in the off phase with `blink-text=full`, dimmed with `partial`. Off, they stay while the text blinks.
#no-blink-decorations

## The longest a synchronized frame (mode 2026) is held before it is shown as drawn; 0 offers no mode 2026.
#sync-timeout=200

## Turn the displays off after this long with no key press or pointer action (a move, a button or the wheel); 0 never does.
#dpms-timeout=600

## Let a program set the blank's timeout with the Linux console's `CSI 9 ; n ]` (n minutes, at most 60; 0 never blanks) and wake the displays with `CSI 13 ]`, as `setterm --blank` does under `TERM=linux`; RIS returns the timeout to `dpms-timeout`. Off, the sequences are ignored. A key press or pointer action wakes the displays either way.
#program-blank

## At start, how long to wait for a display, counted from when the VT is first shown; with none by then vtium exits with status 1, so the unit's `OnFailure=` can start a getty. Under a head file whose policy is closed vtium waits however long it takes, as under `forever` (ADR-1401 D8). 0 waits for no display to come: a display set up when the VT is first shown counts once its first frame is on screen. `forever` waits for the first display however long it takes, for a machine whose only display is a USB adapter (DisplayLink, `udl`) plugged in at any time. Once a display has come, vtium never exits for want of one.
#display-wait=10

## How many buffers each DRM display keeps, the same for every display: `3`, triple buffering, so no frame waits for the last flip, at the cost of one more whole frame a display (8 MB at 1080p); `2`, double buffering; `1`, single buffering, which holds the least memory, but vtium then draws into the buffer being shown, so a frame can be seen half drawn. A framebuffer (fbdev) keeps one buffer whatever this key says. Unset, the build decides: 3 in a build with features on top of minspec, 2 in the minspec build, 1 only in a build smaller than minspec.
#display-buffers=

## Draw through the GPU, in a build that has it.
#no-hwaccel

## Keep the mode each display is in; off, its preferred mode.
#no-use-original-mode

## A display's mode, `WxH[@RATE]`, for every display, `[CONNECTOR]WxH` for one connector, or `GPU[CONNECTOR]WxH` for one on one card, its udev `ID_PATH`. A key per line; the most specific match wins. Unset, the panel's preferred mode. It turns `use-original-mode` off.
#mode=

## The sixteen colors and the default foreground and background: a named palette, or `custom`, which takes the `palette-*` keys. Colors 16 to 255 are xterm's in every palette.
#palette=legacy

## Color 0 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-black=0,0,0

## Color 1 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-red=205,0,0

## Color 2 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-green=0,205,0

## Color 3 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-yellow=205,205,0

## Color 4 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-blue=0,0,238

## Color 5 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-magenta=205,0,205

## Color 6 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-cyan=0,205,205

## Color 7 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-grey=229,229,229

## Color 8 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-dark-grey=127,127,127

## Color 9 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-red=255,0,0

## Color 10 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-green=0,255,0

## Color 11 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-yellow=255,255,0

## Color 12 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-blue=92,92,255

## Color 13 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-magenta=255,0,255

## Color 14 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-light-cyan=0,255,255

## Color 15 with `palette=custom`.
#palette-white=255,255,255

## The default foreground with `palette=custom`.
#palette-foreground=229,229,229

## The default background with `palette=custom`.
#palette-background=0,0,0

## The color every underline draws in, SGR 4 in each of its styles and SGR 21: `fg` draws it in the text's color, a palette index in that palette color, which follows a program's OSC 4, and `#rrggbb` in that color. A color a program sets with SGR 58 wins unless `force-decoration-colors` is on.
#underline-color=fg

## The color the strike, SGR 9, draws in, as `underline-color` takes it.
#strike-color=fg

## `underline-color` wins over the underline color a program sets with SGR 58.
#no-force-decoration-colors

## Take the selection a program sets with OSC 52; off, it is dropped. The console has no system clipboard: vtium holds the last selection taken, as it holds one the user copies, and the middle button and `grab-paste` paste it.
#clipboard-write

## The keyboard model, as xkeyboard-config names it.
#xkb-model=

## The keyboard layout, such as `us` or `de`.
#xkb-layout=

## The layout's variant.
#xkb-variant=

## xkb options, comma-separated.
#xkb-options=

## A keymap file, taken before the names above.
#xkb-keymap=

## A compose table, taken before the locale's.
#xkb-compose-file=

## How long a held key waits before it repeats; 0 takes 250.
#xkb-repeat-delay=250

## The time between a held key's repeats; 0 takes 50.
#xkb-repeat-rate=50

## Take pointers: mice, touchpads and touchscreens.
#mouse

## Invert the mouse wheel: it scrolls the view and reports to a program the other way, and so do the cursor keys it sends on the alternate screen under mode 1007. Ctrl and the wheel still zoom in when the wheel turns up.
#no-natural-scrolling

## Draw the pointer in software on every display, never on a display's cursor plane.
#no-soft-cursor

## Draw the pointer as this character's glyph from the font, one cell wide, on the cell under the pointer, white with a dark outline as the arrow is. Unset, or a character the font lacks or whose glyph is blank, the pointer is the arrow.
#pointer-char=

## Hide the pointer after this long with no pointer event, until it next moves; 0 never hides it.
#pointer-hide-timeout=20

## Hide the pointer at each key sent to the program, until it next moves. A key vtium takes, such as a grab, leaves it.
#no-pointer-hide-while-typing

## Paste the selection buffer to the program, once a press, as the middle button does. A chord with Shift names the keysym Shift makes: `<Ctrl><Shift>V`, not `<Ctrl><Shift>v`. Empty, no key pastes.
#grab-paste=<Shift>Insert

## Reboot the machine. With `/proc/sys/kernel/ctrl-alt-del` at 0, init is asked, as for Ctrl+Alt+Del, so the services stop first; otherwise the kernel restarts at once. Unset, no key reboots.
#grab-reboot=

## Power the machine off: init is asked with `SIGRTMIN+4`, which systemd takes as `poweroff.target`, so the services stop and the filesystems are unmounted first. Unset, no key powers off.
#grab-power-off=

## Suspend the machine to RAM: logind is asked with its `Suspend` method, so its inhibitors hold the sleep; where logind is not running, or vtium is built without `logind`, `mem` is written to `/sys/power/state`. Unset, no key suspends.
#grab-suspend=

## The font source to use. Unset, each the build has, in this order.
#font-engine=

## The font: a frit's name, `NAME.vtfrit` in `/etc/vtium/fonts`, whose glyphs the helper generates at run time; a kbd font's name, or with `font-engine=psf` a PSF font's path, that no frit bears becomes a one-source frit. Unset, vconsole's font; with none, or a name that is neither, vtium draws its built-in fonts and logs the name and why.
#font-name=monospace

## The directory of the frits, the `NAME.vtfrit` files that `font-name` and a head's profile name. The default is the directory vtium was built with.
#font-frits=/etc/vtium/fonts

## The glyph helper's program, which vtium starts to draw a frit's glyphs, as `nobody` when vtium runs as root. vtium opens it once at start and starts every helper from that file, so a helper installed later reaches a console when the console starts again. vtium refuses a path that is not absolute, a file that is not a regular file, a setuid or setgid file, a file owned by an account other than root or the account vtium runs as, and a file its group or others may write; it logs why and draws its built-in font. It starts the helper through `/proc/self/fd`, so `/proc` must be mounted. The default is the path vtium was built with.
#font-helper=/usr/bin/vtium-vitrify

## The font's size in points, 1/72 inch, drawn at points × DPI ÷ 72 pixels at the displays' density (`display-dpi`), and over a frit's `cell=`; 0 picks one from the display's size.
#font-size=0

## How the font helper is to hint outline glyphs, over a frit's `hinting=`: `light` fits each glyph to the pixel grid up and down only; `full` fits a TrueType font both ways, and draws a CFF font as `light` does. Bitmap fonts take no hinting. vtium does not yet hand this key to the helper, so for now each frit's `hinting=` decides, `light` where it states none.
#font-hinting=

## Height added to each row, over a frit's `line-spacing=`: a whole percentage of the text's height, rounded to whole pixels and split evenly above and below the text, so the grid has fewer rows. It adds nothing to a frit that states `cell=`. Unset, the frit's `line-spacing=` decides, 0 where it states none.
#font-line-spacing=

## Let a program switch the font among the frits and the built-in fonts with xterm's OSC 50 (`OSC 50 ; NAME ST`, as `vtium-vitrify set NAME` sends it) and ask which is in use (`OSC 50 ; ? ST`). On by default, as xterm's `allowFontOps` is, since OSC 50 reaches only the frits root wrote and the built-in fonts, never a file. A switch changes the live console only; `font-name` decides the font at the next start. Off, vtium ignores OSC 50, the query included.
#allow-font-ops

## Where the six sprite ranges draw from: `font`, the font's glyphs; `sprites`, vtium's; `auto`, the font's where it has one. Unset, a named font's glyphs for each range it covers whole and the sprites for the rest; the built-in unifont draws the sprites.
#glyph-source=

## `glyph-source` for box drawing, U+2500-U+257F, over it.
#glyph-source-box=

## `glyph-source` for block elements, U+2580-U+259F, over it.
#glyph-source-block=

## `glyph-source` for Braille, U+2800-U+28FF, over it.
#glyph-source-braille=

## `glyph-source` for sextants, U+1FB00-U+1FB3B, over it.
#glyph-source-sextants=

## `glyph-source` for octants, U+1CD00-U+1CDE5, over it.
#glyph-source-octants=

## `glyph-source` for the rest of Symbols for Legacy Computing's block shapes, over it.
#glyph-source-legacy=

## What a character the font lacks draws as: `box`, U+25A1; `hex`, its codepoint in hex digits.
#missing=box

## The glyph cache's budgets. Unset, `minspec`, or `performance` in a build with vtium-render's `performance-cache` feature.
#glyph-cache=

## Each display's depth. `1` draws it in black and white, each cell lit or unlit by its colors, never by their brightness; `gray4` and `gray16` record a gray panel of 4 or 16 levels, drawn as color, which the panel converts; `color` draws its colors; `auto` takes the levels the display says it shows: an fbdev device at 1 bit, or a DRM panel whose driver vtium-platform lists.
#display-depth=auto

## Each display's density, which converts `font-size` and a frit's `size=` from points to pixels, over the density a display measures. Unset, the density is measured from the physical size the display reports, else assumed at 96 with one logged warning per display.
#display-dpi=

## Measure each display's frames once a second, and write the figures to the log, draw them on screen, or both.
#render-timer=none

## The most bytes each console's pictures hold, its main and alternate screens together, or KiB, MiB or GiB with `K`, `M` or `G` (`64M`), at most 1 TiB. `auto` gives each console four frames of its head's displays, 16 MiB to 192 MiB, and works it out again when a display joins or leaves the head or changes size. `0` turns sixel and kitty graphics off. A build without images reads it and draws no picture.
#image-storage-limit=auto

## Sixel graphics. Off, vtium neither offers nor decodes sixel: DA1 reports no attribute 4 and a sixel string is ignored. vtium offers sixel only while `image-storage-limit` is above 0 and every display of the console's head draws pictures. A build without images never offers it.
#sixel

## The kitty graphics protocol. Off, vtium neither offers nor decodes it: a graphics command is ignored and a query gets no reply. vtium offers it only while `image-storage-limit` is above 0 and every display of the console's head draws pictures. A build without images never offers it.
#kitty-graphics

## The largest picture a program may send, in pixels, `WxH`, for sixel and kitty graphics alike; a larger one is refused. A build without images reads it and draws no picture.
#max-graphic-size=10000x10000

## How many color registers a sixel picture has, and the most XTSMGRAPHICS lets a program set. A build without images reads it and draws no picture.
#num-color-registers=1024

## Mode 1070's state at a reset: on, each sixel picture starts from the default colors; off, the pictures share their color registers. A build without images reads it and draws no picture.
#private-color-registers

## The image helper's program, which vtium starts as the session's user to read the files and shared memory a kitty graphics command names and to decode PNG and zlib-compressed pictures, since vtium itself, running as root, does neither. vtium opens it once at start and starts every helper from that file, so a helper installed later reaches the consoles when vtium starts again. vtium refuses a path that is not absolute, a file that is not a regular file, a setuid or setgid file, a file owned by an account other than root or the account vtium runs as, and a file its group or others may write; it logs why, and every console then answers those pictures itself, a file, a temporary file or shared memory with `EBADF` and PNG or zlib with `EINVAL`. It starts the helper through `/proc/self/fd`, so `/proc` must be mounted. The default is the path vtium was built with. A build without images reads it and starts no helper.
#image-helper=/usr/bin/vtium-image
